React Compiler is enabled in this codebase, so it already auto-memoizes derived values inside components. `data` here is just a plain derived value (an `isOffline` branch plus a `.filter`), which the compiler caches on its own based on `sourceData` and `isOffline`. Wrapping it in `useMemo` is redundant, adds a dependency array to maintain, and can interfere with the compiler's own caching. This file has no other manual memoization, and the sibling `TransactionGroupListItem.tsx` in this same PR relies on the compiler too.

Remove the `useMemo` and compute the value directly:

```tsx
// Deleting every expense in a group flags the group's own snapshot entry, so drop the row from the list and let
// its exit animation play. Offline the row stays put with its pending-delete styling, as elsewhere.
const data = isOffline ? sourceData : sourceData.filter((item) => !isRowDeleted(item));
```

Then drop `useMemo` from the `react` import. (Please confirm the file still passes `npm run react-compiler-compliance-check -- check src/components/Search/ExpenseGroupedSearchView.tsx`; if it fails to compile, manual memoization is acceptable and this can be disregarded.)

