React Compiler is enabled and automatically memoizes closures, so wrapping `unregister` in `useCallback` is redundant. The callback only uses the stable `setEntries` setter via a functional update, so the compiler can cache it without manual help.

Remove the `useCallback` wrapper and define `unregister` as a plain function.

```tsx
const unregister = (slot: MenuItemLabelSlot) => {
    setEntries((prev) => {
        if (!prev.has(slot)) {
            return prev;
        }
        const next = new Map(prev);
        next.delete(slot);
        return next;
    });
};
```

React Compiler is enabled and automatically memoizes closures based on their captured variables, so wrapping `register` in `useCallback` is redundant. The callback only uses the `setEntries` setter (a stable reference) via a functional update, so the compiler can cache it without manual help.

Remove the `useCallback` wrapper and define `register` as a plain function.

```tsx
const register = (slot: MenuItemLabelSlot, value: string) => {
    setEntries((prev) => {
        if (prev.get(slot) === value) {
            return prev;
        }
        const next = new Map(prev);
        next.set(slot, value);
        return next;
    });
};
```

